            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>This Week's Observation: More Accounts, Lower Efficiency</h2>
<p>This week, we heard a common pain point from several overseas ad teams: the more accounts they open, the lower their management efficiency becomes. One app team is currently managing 23 Meta ad accounts across 5 regional markets and 3 product lines with only 4 people. Their biggest issue isn't lack of creatives — it's "too many accounts, and we're unsure what role each one should play."</p>
<p>This is a bottleneck that multi-account teams inevitably face. When account numbers exceed a certain threshold without clear classification, teams fall into chaos: which account is running what, which should be scaled, which should be paused.</p>
<h2>Why Tiered Management Has Become Essential</h2>
<p>Over the past year, tightening platform policies and budget volatility have increased pressure on multi-account teams. Issues like policy violations, budget caps, and unstable delivery force teams to consider: how to diversify risk without sacrificing overall efficiency?</p>
<p>The essence of tiered management is defining clear responsibility boundaries for each account. Different accounts carry different roles with different budgets, optimization frequencies, and risk thresholds. This is far more efficient than "managing all accounts uniformly."</p>
<h2>A Three-Tier Model for Practice</h2>
<p>Based on this week's discussions, here's a relatively universal tiering framework:</p>
<p><strong>Tier 1 — Core Accounts: Stable Revenue Generators</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Volume: 20%~30% of total accounts</li>
<li>Characteristics: Accumulated historical data, stable ROI, high account权重</li>
<li>Management Focus: Maintain stable budgets, avoid frequent adjustments; monitor CPA fluctuations; serve as primary vehicle for new creative testing</li>
<li>Risk Control: Set lower auto-pause thresholds to prevent budget overruns</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Tier 2 — Growth Accounts: Scaling Engines</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Volume: 40%~50% of total accounts</li>
<li>Characteristics: Some data accumulated, but stability and ROI not yet at Tier 1 level</li>
<li>Management Focus: Regular reviews to identify candidates for promotion to Tier 1; test new targeting and bidding strategies; capture traffic that Tier 1 doesn't pursue</li>
<li>Risk Control: Set budget caps to prevent testing from consuming excessive budget</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Tier 3 — Seed Accounts: Low-Cost Experiment Fields</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Volume: 20%~30% of total accounts</li>
<li>Characteristics: New accounts or low-budget accounts, mainly for validating new markets and creative directions</li>
<li>Management Focus: Observation-focused, no short-term ROI pursuit; record experiment data for future scaling reference</li>
<li>Risk Control: Strictly control daily budget per account; pause immediately if anomalies occur</li>
</ul>
<h2>Three Specific Recommendations to Improve Management Efficiency</h2>
<p><strong>1. Build an Account Dashboard Instead of Logging Into Each Account Individually</strong></p>
<p>One efficient team's approach: use spreadsheets or dashboard tools to aggregate core metrics across all accounts (daily spend, ROI, CPA, budget remaining). No need to log into each account daily — a quick dashboard scan tells you which accounts need attention. One cross-border e-commerce team reduced their daily account review time from 90 minutes to 20 minutes using this method.</p>
<p><strong>2. Set Tiered Review Frequencies</strong></p>
<p>Not all accounts need daily checking. Tier 1 accounts: twice daily (morning and evening); Tier 2: once daily; Tier 3: every two to three days. Focus high-frequency reviews on Tier 1 to ensure core channel stability.</p>
<p><strong>3.分层预算调整权限</strong></p>
<p>Set different budget adjustment permissions for different tiers. Tier 1 adjustments require supervisor confirmation; Tier 2 operators can adjust directly; Tier 3 changes require documented reasons. This balances flexibility with risk control.</p>
<h2>Key Takeaway This Week</h2>
<p>The core challenge of multi-account advertising isn't "how many accounts to open," but "how to make each account do what it should." The key to tiered management is giving your team clear visibility into each account's role, reducing ineffective reviews, and focusing energy on accounts that truly need optimization.</p>
<p>Recommended action this week: map all your current accounts, classify them using the three-tier model above, and clarify each account's positioning and corresponding management strategy. This may improve your overall campaign efficiency more directly than updating creatives.</p>
